Repro: just make a page that renders hundreds of KB of HTML, either during the initial render or during a streaming SSR batch.

Proof-of-concept workaround:

```diff
diff --git a/src/Components/Endpoints/src/Rendering/EndpointHtmlRenderer.cs b/src/Components/Endpoints/src/Rendering/EndpointHtmlRenderer.cs
index bbc46316d9..c29b64c733 100644
--- a/src/Components/Endpoints/src/Rendering/EndpointHtmlRenderer.cs
+++ b/src/Components/Endpoints/src/Rendering/EndpointHtmlRenderer.cs
@@ -214,8 +214,18 @@ internal partial class EndpointHtmlRenderer : StaticHtmlRenderer, IComponentPrer
     {
         if (_streamingUpdatesWriter is { } writer)
         {
-            SendBatchAsStreamingUpdate(renderBatch, writer);
-            return FlushThenComplete(writer, base.UpdateDisplayAsync(renderBatch));
+            using var contentWriter = new StringWriter();
+            SendBatchAsStreamingUpdate(renderBatch, contentWriter);
+            return FlushThenComplete(contentWriter, writer, base.UpdateDisplayAsync(renderBatch));
         }
         else
         {
@@ -224,8 +234,9 @@ internal partial class EndpointHtmlRenderer : StaticHtmlRenderer, IComponentPrer
 
         // Workaround for methods with "in" parameters not being allowed to be async
         // We resolve the "result" first and then combine it with the FlushAsync task here
-        static async Task FlushThenComplete(TextWriter writerToFlush, Task completion)
+        static async Task FlushThenComplete(TextWriter contentToWrite, TextWriter writerToFlush, Task completion)
         {
+            await writerToFlush.WriteAsync(contentToWrite.ToString());
             await writerToFlush.FlushAsync();
             await completion;
         }
```

That workaround only handles the case where the problem occurs during a streaming SSR batch. A similar buffering workaround should work around the initial HTML output.

In our case we had to allow synchronous IO and use IIS Express instead of Kestrel (with Kestrel wouldn't work with AllowSynchronousIO  set to true.

Still looking up for a final solution.

```
builder.Services.Configure<IISServerOptions>(options =>
{
    options.AllowSynchronousIO = true;
});
```
